ucd_dictionary: optional Southeast Asian dictionary segmentation

Source: ICU release-78.1, commit
049e0d6a420629ac7db77256987d083a563287b5
https://github.com/unicode-org/icu/tree/049e0d6a420629ac7db77256987d083a563287b5

The engine is a safe Silk port of PossibleWord, ThaiBreakEngine,
LaoBreakEngine, KhmerBreakEngine and BurmeseBreakEngine from
icu4c/source/common/dictbe.cpp. DictionaryMatcher prefix behavior is from
icu4c/source/common/dictionarydata.cpp. Copyright (C) 2006-2016
International Business Machines Corporation and others; copyright 2016
and later Unicode, Inc. and others. The complete pinned ICU LICENSE is
copied alongside this notice, including Unicode License v3 and the Lao
and Burmese dictionary notices. No Chinese/Japanese dictionary is shipped.

Data inputs are thaidict.txt, laodict.txt, khmerdict.txt and burmesedict.txt
under icu4c/source/data/brkitr/dictionaries. All input SHA256 checksums,
including source, reference tests and license, are pinned in
text/ucd/scripts/generate-unicode-dictionaries. Download/cache location:
build/icu/049e0d6a420629ac7db77256987d083a563287b5/

Regenerate data, license and reference fixtures:
  text/ucd/scripts/generate-unicode-dictionaries
Require the verified cache and prohibit network access:
  text/ucd/scripts/generate-unicode-dictionaries --offline
Check generated-file freshness without writing:
  text/ucd/scripts/generate-unicode-dictionaries --check --offline
Run behavior and pinned upstream reference cases:
  rm -rf build/llvm build/cache
  silk test --release --manifest-path text/ucd/dictionary/silk.toml --root src/tests.silk

Only applications opting into ucd_dictionary link its packed byte-literal
minimal acyclic tries (about 2.9 MB). The ordinary ucd library has no
reverse dependency. Import IcuDictionary from ucd_dictionary.lib, construct
it with IcuDictionary.make(), and use the ucd.dictionary.DictionarySegmenter
interface or pass it to ucd.line_break.withDictionary. Construction is
allocation-free; segmentation allocates only when the caller's output List
needs growth. Three reusable fixed-capacity candidate stacks implement
ICU's ordered three-word lookahead, short-root/unknown-prefix combination,
language-specific resynchronization, mark attachment and Thai suffix rules.
The generator proves that all dictionary candidates fit the 20-slot stacks.

Positions are offsets in the original UTF-8 input. Empty input yields [0];
otherwise output includes 0 and the input length exactly once. The caller
clears output before each call. Each script's Script intersect Line_Break=SA
runs are processed independently using ucd's Unicode 18 properties. Other
spans remain opaque. Dictionary-set edges within same-script SA/AL alphabetic
continuations are not emitted as word boundaries, matching the enclosing
$ALPlus rule in pinned ICU line.txt. Other span edges are exposed. This is
not a general UAX29 word segmenter; use ucd.segmentation for that. Invalid
UTF-8 consumes one byte per U+FFFD, consistently with ucd.utf8. The ICU Thai
minimum-span guard counts scalars; the other engines retain ICU's native-unit
guard, with UTF-8 byte units. This matters for very short standalone runs
when comparing with ICU's UTF-16 rather than UTF-8 UText interface.

The 51 reference cases in generated_reference.silk come from pinned ICU's
icu4c/source/test/testdata/rbbitst.txt, including its Thai spelling, suffix
and city-name regressions, Lao additions, Khmer ambiguity cases and Myanmar
UDHR spans. Source line provenance is retained per fixture. Myanmar fixtures
are entire alphabetic spans between spaces in ICU's line-break cases (including
non-SA alphabetic symbols), not arbitrary truncated words. Expectations are
the upstream reference breaks,
not outputs generated by this port. The generator reproduces these fixtures
without executing or linking native ICU. Native ICU is neither a build nor
runtime dependency. General RBBI punctuation/whitespace rules and ICU LSTM
engines are outside this dictionary engine's API.
